uid "251bb292-8556-5b99-b0e3-0dda73534412" :question/math-academy-id "q-129973" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(1\\le X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-129973/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129973"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection/f4364ee92d59ffa19a35fed1b82b6f9ee1aa78323ca0601a0ef87993ec530d58" :answer/id #uuid "aae0a55a-aacd-5b17-8448-dcdfd9defcfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection/8a1ef262b909bbcc5ce75620f12441a604ca3882ee30bc3d2553941011ab2558" :answer/id #uuid "ddd561ef-5914-5479-ad1c-3dd31cec0a37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3254" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection/1da20998d6e05418eefe52189b1105517e7fba4b5bc0151936c57c54f6345182" :answer/id #uuid "b4ddc0f0-18ee-5c54-9fed-58526e5513a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3783" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection/6a1d6b9b5a72e2853b9f4868b260ea43ca662b2883e193a0775e1c4a38c0c908" :answer/id #uuid "cbd877e5-7438-52b6-bd3c-a6a18a96881b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4960" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection/828ab5bfa53fe7fa9b1ae0051a8a201603d120831ea6e8fb0e6bb36996c52bab" :answer/id #uuid "3234ee43-c2ad-5bcd-9f7f-e98b14b18be3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4520" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "76287ec9-04e3-51b5-ad31-0c438a64ec25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97519/selection/6a1d6b9b5a72e2853b9f4868b260ea43ca662b2883e193a0775e1c4a38c0c908" :answer-field/choices ["q-97519/selection/f4364ee92d59ffa19a35fed1b82b6f9ee1aa78323ca0601a0ef87993ec530d58" "q-97519/selection/8a1ef262b909bbcc5ce75620f12441a604ca3882ee30bc3d2553941011ab2558" "q-97519/selection/1da20998d6e05418eefe52189b1105517e7fba4b5bc0151936c57c54f6345182" "q-97519/selection/6a1d6b9b5a72e2853b9f4868b260ea43ca662b2883e193a0775e1c4a38c0c908" "q-97519/selection/828ab5bfa53fe7fa9b1ae0051a8a201603d120831ea6e8fb0e6bb36996c52bab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97519" :question/id #uuid "3f819a50-9929-519d-9a3a-6f262e5676fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97519" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(7,0.9),$ compute $P(5\\le X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{7-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{7-6} \\\\  & =21(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{2}+7(0.9)^{6}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.4960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-97519/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97519"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection/15e111f1dc476be4a72fb33ad0aaf193d70eccd5f72d20bcf7ef089e232b94f2" :answer/id #uuid "05b38550-6984-5c18-afba-d5ef1e4142ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1376" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection/16661544a7e6d38f4c413b3523ec59007c21136a2a977aef7cb1cb6292873ed2" :answer/id #uuid "0249f93f-3ae5-5560-a2c7-ca865ea72bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection/caca97fdc725a237169304007a63d3f792d13476591ce0f1a09940e924196753" :answer/id #uuid "6ef1f418-4644-5a7c-b4a0-fa8c5423f692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0892" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection/bd6837f4743d16ed430ab35240827c1665d9841639f09216870e53ba3f340028" :answer/id #uuid "da8a30e0-674f-5a6b-be1c-7daad5c1b703" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1927" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection/75add91c8f14e5271abfe38a95bfb16b809813840f1e35de9e09ab43140ab3c5" :answer/id #uuid "caea3a73-9878-5286-9296-5fb702167bce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1560" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c3d77f0-7998-5456-9d63-28021ea72744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97511/selection/bd6837f4743d16ed430ab35240827c1665d9841639f09216870e53ba3f340028" :answer-field/choices ["q-97511/selection/15e111f1dc476be4a72fb33ad0aaf193d70eccd5f72d20bcf7ef089e232b94f2" "q-97511/selection/16661544a7e6d38f4c413b3523ec59007c21136a2a977aef7cb1cb6292873ed2" "q-97511/selection/caca97fdc725a237169304007a63d3f792d13476591ce0f1a09940e924196753" "q-97511/selection/bd6837f4743d16ed430ab35240827c1665d9841639f09216870e53ba3f340028" "q-97511/selection/75add91c8f14e5271abfe38a95bfb16b809813840f1e35de9e09ab43140ab3c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97511" :question/id #uuid "ddfb0f45-b8af-524a-84d2-a0a444691b42" :question/math-academy-id "q-97511" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(8,0.2),$ compute $P(2<X<5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(8,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{8-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<5) & =P(X\\in {3,4}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{8-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{8-4} \\\\  & =56(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{5}+70(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.1927\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97511/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97511"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97516"] :question/problem "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-279767/field-1/3591f3d6caac480182cd3e2b4fcbee80d88c0997604301c1b6f68aa726facb50" :answer/id #uuid "54159eb5-1daa-5887-873f-c149fa6898fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.401" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279767/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4051e47-e068-5c2e-b08b-3d1baaef6a88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279767/field-1/3591f3d6caac480182cd3e2b4fcbee80d88c0997604301c1b6f68aa726facb50" :answer-field/choices ["q-279767/field-1/3591f3d6caac480182cd3e2b4fcbee80d88c0997604301c1b6f68aa726facb50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279767" :question/id #uuid "46475705-95e8-5e1f-99b3-822a4df8c2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-279767" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,0.1),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places** $P(0<X\\le 2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-279767/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279767"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279984/field-1/bae2c3a61e514079150dc98ff9ae196c9f29d04bbe2b24a3bceddee1afab8906" :answer/id #uuid "4c768b3d-749b-593a-97f8-943c0daf9bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.423" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279984/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0931eb65-b00c-55f4-ac00-9ddb13d8c3e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279984/field-1/bae2c3a61e514079150dc98ff9ae196c9f29d04bbe2b24a3bceddee1afab8906" :answer-field/choices ["q-279984/field-1/bae2c3a61e514079150dc98ff9ae196c9f29d04bbe2b24a3bceddee1afab8906"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279984" :question/id #uuid "1b7f280d-8d1f-5737-92f5-31c5c2c5d7d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279984" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,0.8),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<6)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.8),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,5.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=6,7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X\\in {6,7}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.8)^{7}(0.2)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.8)^{6}(0.2)+(0.8)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.577\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =1-P(X\\ge 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.577 \\\\  & =0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-279984/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279984"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279981/field-1/b3a58cfa4d4e0252100eae4457e31291f36fba09289925256c00eb14bbdd2b13" :answer/id #uuid "5c38d3dd-b48e-549a-b5f1-fe1e16bf0ba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.114" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279981/field-1/f52a86de3cd55a1a00d21804b115fc791013e405345231e33a7790949a391cee" :answer/id #uuid "c484769e-cb73-562f-94f7-eab54668f8f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.115" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279981/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ef71f-7403-581a-834e-d2afffa7f218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279981/field-1/b3a58cfa4d4e0252100eae4457e31291f36fba09289925256c00eb14bbdd2b13" :answer-field/choices ["q-279981/field-1/b3a58cfa4d4e0252100eae4457e31291f36fba09289925256c00eb14bbdd2b13" "q-279981/field-1/f52a86de3cd55a1a00d21804b115fc791013e405345231e33a7790949a391cee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279981" :question/id #uuid "0b9ecd4f-c145-54ba-94bf-6399b3176fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-279981" :question/problem "If $X∼B(6,0.9),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<5)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,4.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 5)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=5,6,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 5),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.9)^{5}(0.1)+(0.9)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.886\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =1-P(X\\ge 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.886 \\\\  & ≈0.114\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-279981/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-281208/field-1/8ec04ca859b2fcb216df4231d575689873d5db0676f222908055512b80c04d7a" :answer/id #uuid "428354be-3dff-54eb-b8ef-e602571fd516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.992" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-281208/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e38cf090-c129-585d-904f-06f9124c5eb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-281208/field-1/8ec04ca859b2fcb216df4231d575689873d5db0676f222908055512b80c04d7a" :answer-field/choices ["q-281208/field-1/8ec04ca859b2fcb216df4231d575689873d5db0676f222908055512b80c04d7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-281208" :question/id #uuid "c028ef54-6121-57ea-ad64-cc5a3f2b20f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-281208" :question/problem "If $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 11)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 11)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>11)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=12,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>11),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =P(X\\in {12}) \\\\  & =f(12) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-12} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & ≈1-0.008 \\\\  & =0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-281208/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-281208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection/df79c0a433e78196bd6b6ab59fb492a8dd31f8325ca6733b9c878de9b8c5bbc9" :answer/id #uuid "ab9dd68b-0374-5a25-8888-688422ce46a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9922" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection/624e45c9fd5cb56f57c962ba1563d5ae5db2c3f5e6cbb230040558b5905de558" :answer/id #uuid "ce812fc0-8a8e-56f3-944a-ce3a2ccee787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection/9370789f52cb556e9964b3f91d7e08adf69fdd018182fe159b1ac42f9cf24dc5" :answer/id #uuid "77b39674-f9f5-520b-bca1-022215c8abf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9078" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection/b2c43f727776051caed189ab0b4bcae428d791b30530193eb73a24bc74ed612c" :answer/id #uuid "48bc76ca-d585-5051-93c4-95db3e5175e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9547" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection/76a090edcd5337707dd0b1cb00a1f5d1bfc49116ba8dec0027dfb410fb233c23" :answer/id #uuid "937c1936-005c-5008-bb84-ad0e6c918c90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9625" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "087a2584-3eb2-50ad-a798-03a456251af1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97521/selection/df79c0a433e78196bd6b6ab59fb492a8dd31f8325ca6733b9c878de9b8c5bbc9" :answer-field/choices ["q-97521/selection/df79c0a433e78196bd6b6ab59fb492a8dd31f8325ca6733b9c878de9b8c5bbc9" "q-97521/selection/624e45c9fd5cb56f57c962ba1563d5ae5db2c3f5e6cbb230040558b5905de558" "q-97521/selection/9370789f52cb556e9964b3f91d7e08adf69fdd018182fe159b1ac42f9cf24dc5" "q-97521/selection/b2c43f727776051caed189ab0b4bcae428d791b30530193eb73a24bc74ed612c" "q-97521/selection/76a090edcd5337707dd0b1cb00a1f5d1bfc49116ba8dec0027dfb410fb233c23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97521" :question/id #uuid "ab34ec08-5c79-5227-9a54-244f98a3f56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97521" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(7,0.5),$ compute $P(X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in {7}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97521/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97521"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279858/field-1/5acabd58818f22ca8f7d4e83f9e67b989a7d99f307221269afe82012e5182ca7" :answer/id #uuid "3f9d7644-b5be-5a7b-960b-ad7de7aba74a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.773" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279858/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae5b5b4-4842-5b3e-8b4e-83d1e9f5e302" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279858/field-1/5acabd58818f22ca8f7d4e83f9e67b989a7d99f307221269afe82012e5182ca7" :answer-field/choices ["q-279858/field-1/5acabd58818f22ca8f7d4e83f9e67b989a7d99f307221269afe82012e5182ca7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279858" :question/id #uuid "368019ec-d338-512e-a617-3fb6fd7e61f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279858" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{2}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\ge 3)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 3)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=3,4,5,6,7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X<3)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X<3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+21{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.227\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.227 \\\\  & =0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-279858/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8557"] :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ compute $P(X = 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6-2} \\\\  & =15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0595\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8564"] :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ compute $P(X > 8).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in {9,10}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8558"] :question/problem "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ compute $P(0 <     X < 3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8559"] :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ compute $P(X \\geq 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
